# Update Existing Utility: Generic Multi-Team Automation Monitoring & Onboarding

Update the existing Live Automation Monitoring Utility with the following additional capabilities.

**Important:** Do not rebuild the application from scratch. Inspect the existing codebase, reuse existing classes and functionality, and make only the changes required to support the enhancements below.

Preserve the existing Java 21, Spring Boot 3, Maven, HTML, CSS and Vanilla JavaScript architecture.

## 1. Make the Utility Generic and Multi-Team Compatible

Currently, the utility should not be limited to one application, one project, or a predefined set of automation skills.

Enhance it to support:
- Multiple organizations and teams.
- Multiple projects/applications per team.
- Multiple frontend and backend components per application.
- Multiple existing automation test suites per component.
- Multiple execution runs across projects and environments.
- Independent monitoring and execution history for each project.

Do not hardcode project names, component names, team names, suite names or automation frameworks.

## 2. Introduce Project Onboarding

Add a new Project Onboarding section to the existing dashboard.

Allow authorized users to register an application through the UI.

Project details:
- Project name.
- Unique project ID.
- Description.
- Owning team.
- Application URL (optional).
- Environments such as QA, SIT, UAT and PROD.
- Repository information (optional).

Allow users to add multiple components to a project.

Component details:
- Component name.
- Unique component ID.
- Component type: FRONTEND, BACKEND, API, SERVICE or CUSTOM.
- Description.
- Repository information (optional).

All components must be dynamically configurable.

## 3. Support Existing Automation Test Suite Onboarding

Add a Test Suite Registration section.

Users should be able to register their existing automation test suites and map them to the relevant application components.

Test suite details:
- Suite name.
- Unique suite ID.
- Associated project.
- Associated component.
- Programming language.
- Automation framework.
- Repository information.
- Integration type.
- Description.
- Optional tags.

Examples of supported frameworks:
- Java + JUnit 5.
- Java + TestNG.
- Selenium.
- Rest Assured.
- JavaScript + Playwright.
- JavaScript + Jest.
- Python + Pytest.
- Custom automation frameworks.

A component should support multiple test suites.

Do not force users to recreate their existing test cases in this platform.

The platform should monitor existing test suites, not replace them.

## 4. Introduce a Framework-Independent Integration Mechanism

This is a critical enhancement.

Existing automation scripts must be able to report execution results to the dashboard without rewriting their actual test logic.

Introduce a standardized, versioned reporting API.

Support:
- Generic HTTP REST event reporting.
- JUnit 5 integration as the first working framework adapter.
- GitLab CI/CD reporting integration or a documented pipeline example.
- Extensible adapter architecture for future TestNG, Playwright and Pytest integrations.

Provide an integration setup page for each registered test suite.

It should display:
- Dashboard API endpoint.
- Project ID.
- Component ID.
- Suite ID.
- Integration instructions.
- Credential configuration guidance.
- Sample reporting payload.
- Integration validation status.

Use secure, revocable integration credentials. Never expose secrets in frontend code or logs.

## 5. Standardize Execution Events

Extend the existing event model to support project, component, suite, run and test-case-level execution tracking.

Use a common event envelope containing:
- schemaVersion.
- eventId.
- eventType.
- projectId.
- componentId.
- suiteId.
- runId.
- testCaseId (optional).
- status.
- timestamp.
- message.
- metadata.

Support these event types:

Run:
- RUN_STARTED.
- RUN_PROGRESS.
- RUN_COMPLETED.
- RUN_FAILED.
- RUN_ABORTED.

Suite:
- SUITE_STARTED.
- SUITE_PROGRESS.
- SUITE_COMPLETED.
- SUITE_FAILED.
- SUITE_SKIPPED.

Test case:
- TEST_STARTED.
- TEST_PASSED.
- TEST_FAILED.
- TEST_SKIPPED.
- TEST_ABORTED.

Logs:
- LOG_MESSAGE.
- ERROR_MESSAGE.
- ARTIFACT_REPORTED.

Validate event ownership, identifiers, event ordering, timestamps, payload size and supported schema version.

Implement event ID-based idempotency to prevent duplicate results.

Support both:
1. Live event reporting while tests are running.
2. Final test report ingestion when the existing automation framework only provides results after execution.

Do not assume every framework supports live test-level progress.

